@startuml provision-sequence
skinparam dpi 140
skinparam roundCorner 8
skinparam noteFontSize 9
skinparam sequenceMessageFontSize 9
skinparam sequenceParticipantFontSize 10
skinparam defaultFontSize 9
skinparam defaultFontName Helvetica
skinparam responseMessageBelowArrow true

title EAP-DID Enrolment (Credential Issuance)\nFigure 4 -- Trust Model D: the Issuer DID is the only identifier published to the VDR

box "Issuing Authority" #E3F2FD
    participant "Issuer Agent" as issuer
    participant "HTTP Proxy\n(Issuer)" as caddy_i
end box
box "Subject System" #E8F5E9
    participant "HTTP Proxy\n(Holder)" as caddy_h
    participant "Holder Agent" as holder
end box
database "Verifiable Data\nRegistry" as bc

note over bc
  <b>VDR</b>. Holds the Issuer DID document and credential
  status lists. Any Verifier resolves the Issuer here
  without contacting the Issuing Authority.
  --
  Under Model D no subject identifier is published.
end note

== 1. Identifier Creation ==

issuer -> issuer : POST /did-registrar/dids\n{"documentTemplate":{"publicKeys":[\n  {"id":"issuanceKey","purpose":"assertionMethod"}]}}
issuer -> issuer : state CREATED\ndid:prism:<hash>:<create operation>
issuer -> bc : POST /did-registrar/dids/{did}/publications

note over issuer, bc #FFFDE7
  <b>DID publication (Issuer only).</b> The DID create operation is
  anchored to the ledger. State transitions CREATED ->
  PUBLICATION_PENDING -> PUBLISHED; settlement is on the order of
  10 to 30 seconds and depends on the ledger slot time.

  Once published the identifier is referenced in its short form,
  did:prism:<hash>, since the document no longer needs to travel
  with the identifier.
end note

bc --> issuer : PUBLISHED, did:prism:<hash>

holder -> holder : POST /did-registrar/dids\n{"documentTemplate":{"publicKeys":[\n  {"id":"authKey","purpose":"authentication"}]}}
holder -> holder : state CREATED\n<b>retained in long form; not published</b>

note over holder, bc #DDF0FF
  <b>Long form resolution</b>

  A did:prism long form is did:prism:<hash>:<create operation>,
  where <create operation> is the base64url-encoded document that
  defines the identifier. Resolution is performed from the
  identifier alone, with no VDR access:

    resolve(long_form):
      hash, op = split(long_form)
      if SHA-256(op) != hash: reject
      return public keys of decode(op)

  The hash binds the document to the identifier, so an unpublished
  identifier is no less verifiable than a published one.

  Consequences under Model D:

  o  No subject identifier is published, so none can be resolved,
     correlated or enumerated by a third party.
  o  Presentation validation is unaffected: the subject key is
     recovered from the identifier itself.
  o  Two authentication exchanges cannot be linked through the VDR.

  The "authKey" key is secp256k1 and is the key that will sign
  presentations using ES256K [RFC8812].
end note

== 2. DIDComm Connection Establishment ==

issuer -> issuer : POST /connections {"label":"issuer-to-holder"}
issuer -> issuer : construct OOB invitation
issuer --> caddy_i : 200 {invitationUrl, thid}
caddy_i --> caddy_h : invitationUrl
caddy_h --> holder : invitationUrl

note over issuer, holder #FFE8C0
  <b>Out-of-band invitation</b> [DIDComm v2]

    _oob = base64url({
      "type":"https://didcomm.org/out-of-band/2.0/invitation",
      "id":"<uuid>",
      "from":"<issuer did:peer>",
      "body":{"goal_code":"connect","accept":["didcomm/v2"]}})

  The "from" value is a did:peer:2 carrying its key material
  inline. It is not published to the VDR.
end note

holder -> holder : POST /connection-invitations\n{"invitation":"<b64url oob>"}
holder -> holder : state InvitationReceived
holder -> issuer : DIDComm ConnectionRequest (JWE)

note over holder, issuer #FFE8C0
  <b>Enrolment channel encryption</b>
  alg = "ECDH-ES+A256KW" [RFC7518]

    (eph_sk, eph_pk) = X25519 keygen [RFC7748]
    Z             = X25519(eph_sk, recipient_pk)
    KEK           = Concat KDF with SHA-256 over Z
    CEK           = random()
    encrypted_key = AES Key Wrap(KEK, CEK) [RFC3394]
    ciphertext    = AEAD(CEK, ConnectionRequest)

  ECDH-ES derives the KEK from a single ephemeral-static
  agreement. It provides confidentiality against third parties
  but no assurance of origin: any party may encrypt to a
  published public key. This is sufficient for enrolment, where
  origin is established by the enclosing protocol.

  The authentication exchange uses ECDH-1PU instead, which
  additionally incorporates the sender's static key. See Figure 3,
  Section 3.3.
end note

issuer -> issuer : receiveConnectionRequest;\nacceptConnectionRequest\nstate ConnectionResponseSent
issuer -> holder : DIDComm ConnectionResponse (JWE)

note over issuer #FFF3E0
  <b>Implementation note.</b> The connection may remain in
  ConnectionResponseSent. This does not block issuance: the
  Holder Agent creates its peer DID independently.
end note

holder -> holder : receiveConnectionResponse

== 3. Credential Schema ==

issuer -> issuer : POST /schema-registry/schemas\n{"name":"eduroam-cred-<timestamp>",\n "version":"1.0.0", "author":"<issuer DID>",\n "schema":{...}}
issuer --> issuer : 200 {guid}\nschemaId = .../schemas/{guid}/schema

note over issuer #E3F2FD
  The schema fixes the credential shape, here
  {name: string, institution: string}. The Verifier names this
  schemaId in the presentation request and checks the credential
  against it during validation.
end note

== 4. Credential Issuance ==

issuer -> issuer : POST /issue-credentials/credential-offers\n{"claims":{...}, "credentialFormat":"JWT",\n "issuingDID":"<issuer DID>", "schemaId":"<schemaId>"}
issuer -> holder : DIDComm OfferCredential
holder -> holder : state OfferReceived
holder -> issuer : POST /accept-offer\n{"subjectId":"<holder long form>"}

note over holder, issuer #DDF0FF
  The subjectId is the long form identifier. This value becomes
  the "sub" claim of the credential, and the peer reads the
  identity under which it signs presentations from that claim
  rather than from configuration, so the two cannot diverge.
end note

issuer -> issuer : issue credential

note over issuer #E3F2FD
  <b>Verifiable Credential, JWT serialization</b>
  [RFC7519] [VC-DATA-MODEL]

  Protected header:
    {"alg":"ES256K", "typ":"JWT",
     "kid":"<issuer DID>#issuanceKey"}

  Claims:
    iss  <issuer DID>            short form; published to the VDR
    sub  <holder long form>      not published
    vc   { "@context":[...],
           "type":["VerifiableCredential"],
           "credentialSchema":{"id":"<schemaId>"},
           "credentialStatus":{...},
           "credentialSubject":{"name":..., "institution":...} }

  Signature: ECDSA over secp256k1 with SHA-256 [RFC8812], encoded
  as R || S, 32 octets each, with S normalised to the lower half
  of the group order.

  ES256K rather than EdDSA: did:prism keys are secp256k1.

  A Verifier resolves "iss" from the VDR, obtains issuanceKey and
  verifies. <b>The Issuer is not contacted again.</b>
end note

issuer -> holder : DIDComm IssueCredential
holder -> holder : state CredentialReceived

== 5. Peer Provisioning ==

note over holder #E8F5E9
  <b>Material transferred to the EAP peer</b>

  1. The credential (JWT).

  2. The secp256k1 private key of "authKey", which signs
     presentations. It is not stored directly: the agent derives
     it from the wallet seed by hierarchical derivation
     m/29'/29'/<did_index>'/<usage>'/<key_index>'. The derived
     public key is checked against the DID create operation before
     the private key is released.

  3. The peer did:peer:2 and its X25519 key pair, used by the
     DIDComm layer during authentication (ECDH-1PU).

  On a production device no extraction occurs: the wallet retains
  the keys and performs signatures on request. Extraction here
  permits the EAP peer to operate without a local agent.
end note

== 6. Resulting State ==

note over issuer, bc #DFFFD8
  <b>Enrolment complete</b>

  |= Artefact|= Form|= Published|
  | Issuer DID | did:prism:<hash> (short form) | <b>yes</b> |
  | Subject DID | did:prism:<hash>:<op> (long form) | <b>no</b> |
  | Peer DID (subject) | did:peer:2 | no |
  | Schema | URI in the Issuer registry | no |
  | Credential | JWT, ES256K, issued by the Issuer | no |
  | Status list | published by the Issuer | referenced |

  Subsequent authentication exchanges contact neither the Issuer
  nor the Holder Agent. See Figure 3.
end note

@enduml
